lammatory condition** (e.g., aggressively lowering blood pressure in malignant hypertension or systemic steroids for inflammatory etiologies), which resolves the fluid leakage and allows the retina to reattach spontaneously.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 59-old male with a history of smoking and hypercholesterolemia presents to the clinic after experiencing a sudden, painless episode of complete vision loss in the left eye that lasted 42 minutes before resolving completely. Clinical details include:<br>• Patient description: <b>Temporary dark shade or curtain that descended from the top of the eye to the bottom, remained for a few minutes, and then slowly rolled back up</b><br>• Ophthalmic examination: <b>Entirely normal, visual acuity 20/20 in both eyes, flat maculae, no active vascular emboli seen</b><br>• Neurologic examination: <b>No focal deficits</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?', '["Amaurosis fugax; urgent carotid duplex ultrasound and echocardiogram", "Temporal arteritis; immediate temporal artery biopsy and systemic high-dose steroids", "Migraine with aura; reassurance and outpatient neurology follow-up", "Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment; immediate dilated funduscopy and scleral buckle", "Central retinal vein occlusion; urgent optical coherence tomography (OCT)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The presentation of sudden, painless, transient monocular vision loss described as a **''curtain descending and ascending''** over the eye, which resolves completely within minutes, is the classic description of **amaurosis fugax** (transient monocular blindness). This is a form of transient ischemic attack (TIA) affecting the eye, most commonly caused by **microemboli (e.g., cholesterol Hollenhorst plaques)** that temporarily occlude the ophthalmic or central retinal artery before dissolving or passing. The most common source of these emboli is a stenotic **atherosclerotic plaque at the bifurcation of the ipsilateral common carotid artery**. Because amaurosis fugax is a highly predictive warning sign for an impending major cerebral stroke or permanent retinal infarction, the most urgent next step is to obtain a **carotid duplex ultrasound** to evaluate for carotid stenosis, along with an **echocardiogram** to rule out cardioembolic sources (e.g., atrial fibrillation, valvular disease).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-old female with dry age-related macular degeneration (AMD) in both eyes presents with a 6-week history of sudden onset of visual distortion in their right eye, reporting that straight lines appear wavy and bent (metamorphopsia). Clinical workup reveals:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>Dropped from a baseline of 20/40 to 20/200 in the affected eye</b><br>• Dilated funduscopy: <b>Grey-green subretinal membrane in the macula associated with a localized subretinal hemorrhage and lipid exudates</b><br>• Optical coherence tomography (OCT): <b>Subretinal fluid and choroidal neovascularization (CNV)</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the first-line treatment?', '["Neovascular (wet) age-related macular degeneration (AMD); prompt intravitreal anti-VEGF injections (e.g., aflibercept or ranibizumab)", "Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment; urgent pneumatic retinopexy", "Central retinal artery occlusion; ocular massage and carbogen therapy", "Central serous chorioretinopathy; observation and lifestyle modification", "Non-neovascular (dry) AMD; daily supplementation with high-dose AREDS2 vitamins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient is presenting with the acute conversion of dry AMD to **neovascular (wet) AMD**, which is characterized by the sudden onset of **metamorphopsia** (distortion of straight lines, classically checked with an Amsler grid) and central vision loss. The underlying pathophysiology is **choroidal neovascularization (CNV)**, where abnormal new blood vessels sprout from the choriocapillaris, penetrate Bruch''s membrane, and enter the sub-RPE or subretinal space. Because these vessels are fragile and lack tight junctions, they leak fluid, lipids, and blood, leading to subretinal hemorrhage and rapid disciform scarring if untreated. First-line therapy consists of **prompt intravitreal injections of anti-vascular endothelial growth factor (anti-VEGF)** agents (such as aflibercept, ranibizumab, or bevacizumab) to inhibit vessel growth, promo